          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My inhaler technique was never checked and I have had two courses of steroids this year. Is that a marker of poor control, and what does guidance describe about stepping up?",
          "answer": "It is important to have your inhaler technique checked, especially since you have had two courses of steroids this year. Not assessing your inhaler technique can significantly affect your asthma control. As noted in the \"Global respiratory strategy report and national asthma panel guidance,\" both adherence to medication and proper inhaler technique are crucial factors in managing asthma.\n\nHaving two courses of steroids in a year may indicate that your asthma is not well controlled. The guidance states that treatment should be stepped up based on your symptoms and history of exacerbations. A clinician would review your current symptom control and exacerbation history to determine the appropriate treatment step. \n\nWhen considering stepping up treatment, the guidelines recommend checking inhaler technique and adherence first, as improving these factors might resolve treatment failures without needing to change your medication. The stepwise approach includes lower steps like as-needed inhaled corticosteroids, middle steps like low-dose corticosteroids with long-acting bronchodilators, and higher steps for more severe cases. It is advisable to discuss these concerns and any necessary adjustments to your treatment plan with your healthcare provider.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
